<TopicPage slug="subjects/math/foundational/euclidean-geometry">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Around 300 BCE in Alexandria, Euclid wrote the <em>Elements</em> — and with it invented a
    new kind of knowledge. The book does not begin with results; it begins with five
    <strong>axioms</strong> (Euclid called them postulates): innocent, self-evident statements
    such as "a straight line can be drawn between any two points" and "all right angles are
    equal". Everything else — the 465 theorems of the <em>Elements</em>, including results you
    will use in engineering, architecture and physics — is <strong>proven</strong>: each
    statement follows logically from the axioms and from earlier theorems, with every step
    visible and checkable. Nothing is believed on Euclid's authority; you can re-derive every
    claim yourself with a compass and a straightedge. Geometry is where humanity invented the
    idea that truth can be <em>earned</em>, step by visible step — the template that every
    mathematical subject since has copied.
  </p>
  <p>
    The method is best understood by watching it work. Below, the classic isosceles triangle
    theorem — two equal sides force two equal base angles — is proved in four moves from
    nothing but a construction and a congruence rule. Notice what a proof gives you that a
    measurement never could: it works for <em>every</em> isosceles triangle, including ones
    too small, too large or too skewed to draw. That leap from "the ones I checked" to "all of
    them" is exactly what makes mathematics different from every empirical science.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="a classic proof, honestly constructed — the isosceles triangle theorem" widget="equationStep" config={proofCfg} />

  <h2>Geometrical terms and the rules of angles</h2>
  <p>
    Euclid first fixes the vocabulary, and precision here pays for itself.
    A <strong>point</strong> has position but no size; a <strong>line</strong> is infinitely
    long and perfectly straight; a <strong>plane</strong> is a flat surface; a
    <strong>segment</strong> is the finite piece between two points; a <strong>ray</strong>
    starts at a point and runs forever. An <strong>angle</strong> measures rotation between two
    rays, in degrees (a full turn = 360°, inherited from Babylonian base-60 astronomy). The
    basic angle facts are few and mighty. Angles on a straight line sum to <strong>180°</strong>;
    angles around a point sum to <strong>360°</strong>; <strong>vertically opposite</strong>
    angles (the X made by two crossing lines) are equal; complementary angles sum to 90° and
    supplementary to 180°.
  </p>
  <p>
    With <strong>parallel lines</strong> crossed by a transversal, three relationships do all
    the work: <strong>corresponding</strong> angles are equal (the F-shape),
    <strong>alternate</strong> angles are equal (the Z-shape), and <strong>co-interior</strong>
    angles sum to 180° (the C-shape). From these, one theorem falls out immediately: the
    angles of any triangle sum to 180° — draw a line through one vertex parallel to the
    opposite side, and the triangle's three angles rearrange themselves into a straight line.
    Push the same argument around an n-sided polygon and the interior angles sum to
    (n − 2) × 180°: 180° for a triangle, 360° for a quadrilateral, 540° for a pentagon.
    Every fact here is proven, not asserted — which is why they hold everywhere, on every
    drawing, forever.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "a proof is just a careful drawing." A diagram is a guide
    for the eye; the proof must not depend on how the diagram happens to look. The isosceles
    proof above works for triangles you could never draw accurately, and it never once
    measures anything.
  </p>

  <h2>Congruence: when two figures are identical</h2>
  <p>
    Two figures are <strong>congruent</strong> if one can be placed exactly on the other by
    sliding, rotating or flipping — same size, same shape. The practical genius of Euclid is
    that you never need to check everything: triangles are pinned down by just three facts.
    <strong>SSS</strong> (three sides), <strong>SAS</strong> (two sides and the angle between
    them), <strong>ASA</strong> (two angles and the side between them) — each guarantees
    congruence, and this is why the widget's proof can stop after one side–angle–side
    comparison. (RHS — right angle, hypotenuse, side — is a special case; and
    <strong>ASS</strong> famously does <em>not</em> work: two different triangles can share
    two sides and a non-included angle.) Congruence is the engine of proof: to show two
    pieces of a figure are equal, carve the figure into congruent triangles — the move Euclid
    uses hundreds of times.
  </p>

  <h2>Similarity: same shape, different size</h2>
  <p>
    Two figures are <strong>similar</strong> if they have the same shape — equal matching
    angles and sides in the same ratio — without necessarily the same size. Every map is a
    similar copy of its territory; every architectural model is a similar copy of its
    building. The scale factor k drives a beautiful hierarchy: lengths multiply by k,
    <strong>areas by k²</strong>, <strong>volumes by k³</strong>. That asymmetry is why a dog
    twice the size of a terrier is not twice as heavy-looking but eight times as heavy, why
    a cake recipe for a 30 cm tin is not twice the recipe for a 15 cm tin but four times it,
    and why elephants have thick legs while ants do not: weight grows as the cube while bone
    strength grows as the square, so giant versions of small animals run out of strength.
    Similar triangles are also the surveyor's oldest tool: the ratio of shadow to height is
    the same for a stick and a pyramid, which is how Thales reportedly measured the Great
    Pyramid around 600 BCE, and how you can find a river's width without crossing it —
    matching angles make matching triangles, and matching triangles make unknown distances
    computable.
  </p>

  <h2>Symmetry: transformations that leave things unchanged</h2>
  <p>
    A figure has <strong>line (reflectional) symmetry</strong> if some line folds it onto
    itself — an isosceles triangle has one such axis, a square four, a circle infinitely many.
    It has <strong>rotational symmetry</strong> of order n if it looks the same after each
    1/n of a full turn — a square has order 4, a regular pentagon 5. Symmetry is more than
    prettiness: it is the deep principle that <em>something survives a change</em>. In the
    isosceles proof, reflection symmetry is exactly why the base angles must match; in
    physics, conservation laws arise from symmetries of space and time (Noether's theorem);
    in chemistry, molecular symmetry predicts spectra. Learning to <em>see</em> symmetry in
    school geometry is the first training in using it as a tool.
  </p>

  <h2>Circle theorems: the crown of school geometry</h2>
  <p>
    Circles generate the most famous theorem-pack in mathematics, and each result follows
    from the same move: an angle subtended (propped open) at the circumference versus the
    same arc seen from the centre.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Angle at the centre is twice the angle at the circumference</strong> standing on the same arc. Every other result is a corollary.</li>
    <li><strong>The angle in a semicircle is 90°</strong> — the centre-angle is 180°, so any triangle inscribed with the diameter as one side is right-angled (Thales' theorem).</li>
    <li><strong>Angles in the same segment are equal</strong> — every point on the same arc sees the chord under the same angle; that is why inscribed angles "sweep" equally.</li>
    <li><strong>Opposite angles of a cyclic quadrilateral sum to 180°</strong> — the four corners of any quadrilateral inscribed in a circle.</li>
    <li><strong>A tangent is perpendicular to the radius</strong> at the point of contact — the radius is the shortest line to the circle, so it must meet the tangent at 90°.</li>
    <li><strong>The alternate segment theorem:</strong> the angle between a tangent and a chord equals the angle the chord subtends in the opposite segment — the fact behind how sound and light "leave" curved surfaces.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    A worked taste of the method: take a triangle inscribed in a circle with one side the
    diameter. The angle at the centre, standing on that diameter, is a straight 180°. The
    centre-angle theorem says the angle at the circumference is half of it — 90°. No
    measurement, no protractor: the right angle is earned in two lines, and it holds for
    <em>every</em> point on the semicircle at once. That is the payoff of the Euclidean
    method — many facts from one argument.
  </p>
  <p>
    One more proof shows the theorems combining. Take a quadrilateral inscribed in a
    circle (a <strong>cyclic quadrilateral</strong>) and ask why its opposite angles sum
    to 180°. Label the vertices A, B, C, D and let angle ADC subtend arc ABC. The angle
    at the centre standing on that same arc is twice angle ADC — and the <em>reflex</em>
    angle at the centre (the rest of the full 360°) is twice angle ABC, since angle ABC
    stands on the other arc. So angle ADC + angle ABC = half of 360° = 180°. Two
    applications of one theorem, an instant corollary — and notice the proof never
    measured anything. This is also how exam problems in this territory are designed:
    almost every "find the angle" puzzle is a two-step chain through these six results,
    and the skill being trained is choosing which theorem links the angles you have to
    the angle you want.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The five axioms deserve their own scrutiny, because four of them behave and the fifth
    doesn't. Axioms 1–4 (draw a line; extend a line; draw a circle; all right angles are
    equal) feel airtight. The fifth — the <strong>parallel postulate</strong>, that through a
    point off a line there is exactly one parallel line — feels like it should be provable
    from the others, and for twenty centuries mathematicians tried. The failures ended in the
    1820s–40s when Lobachevsky, Bolyai and Gauss built <strong>hyperbolic geometry</strong>
    (infinitely many parallels, triangles whose angles sum to <em>less</em> than 180°) and
    Riemann built <strong>elliptic geometry</strong> (no parallels; on a sphere, triangles can
    have angles summing to more than 180° — fly a plane along a great-circle triangle and
    check). Both are fully consistent: if Euclid's geometry contains a contradiction, so do
    they, and vice versa. The parallel postulate is not a truth of logic but a
    <em>choice</em>, and the universe we live in turns out to have made a non-Euclidean one —
    Einstein's general relativity describes gravity as spacetime curvature, geometry in the
    Riemannian family. GPS satellites correct for it daily.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two deeper footnotes. First, Euclid's axioms had quiet gaps — he assumed, without saying,
    that a line has points between any two of its points — and it took David Hilbert in 1899
    to supply a complete, airtight axiom set for plane geometry, closing the subject's
    foundations twenty-three centuries later. Second, the straightedge-and-compass tradition
    is secretly about <strong>constructible numbers</strong>: with compass and straightedge you
    can bisect any angle, construct any rational multiple, and solve quadratics — but trisect
    an arbitrary angle, square the circle or double the cube? Proven impossible in the 1800s,
    by showing each construction would force algebra (specifically, degree-2 field extensions
    and, for the circle, the transcendence of π) that no finite chain of compass moves can
    reach. Even the limits of the method were finally proved — by the method's own descendants.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Euclid, Elements (c. 300 BCE); Heath's translation and commentary; Greenberg, Euclidean and Non-Euclidean Geometries; Hilbert, Foundations of Geometry (1899).</p>
</TopicPage>
